window.CARDS=(window.CARDS||[]).concat([{"t":"Kaizen","d":"Japanese word for continuous improvement","g":4,"p":"Kaizen means continuous improvement through frequent, practical changes involving the people who perform the work.","e":"Example: A hospital clinic applies Kaizen while reviewing patient check-in. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect Kaizen to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Kaizen with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Continuous and Disruptive Change","Disruptive Changes","Continuous Improvement Principles"],"w":"Frequent small improvements build capability and momentum without waiting for a large formal project."},{"t":"Continuous and Disruptive Change","d":"Continuous changes are mostly incremental. They do not necessarily eliminate major costs, but when taken together, small changes can add up to big savings.","g":4,"p":"Continuous change improves an existing system in smaller steps. Disruptive change replaces major assumptions, technology, or ways of working.","e":"Example: Before changing sample processing, leaders at a laboratory explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Continuous and Disruptive Change to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Change Management","Continuous Improvement Principles","Disruptive Changes"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Disruptive Changes","d":"Differ from continuous changes in that they are less frequent, more costly, and may have a significant impact on the way in which work is accomplished.","g":4,"p":"Differ from continuous changes in that they are less frequent, more costly, and may have a significant impact on the way in which work is accomplished.","e":"Example: Before changing ticket resolution, leaders at a customer-support center explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Disruptive Changes to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Continuous and Disruptive Change","Kaizen","Continuous Improvement Principles"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Continuous Improvement Principles","d":"Continuous improvement is supported by strategic alignment, leadership approval, an efficiency-focused culture, visible success stories, formal structure, measurement, and employee participation.","g":4,"p":"Continuous improvement is supported by strategic alignment, leadership approval, an efficiency-focused culture, visible success stories, formal structure, measurement, and employee participation.","e":"Example: A warehouse applies Continuous Improvement Principles while reviewing order fulfillment. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Connect Continuous Improvement Principles to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Continuous Improvement Principles with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories","CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan","d":"To ensure that the continuous improvement of the organization’s processes and activities are central to is sustainability, Continuous Improvement must be incorporated into the strategic plan.","g":4,"p":"This principle says continuous improvement must include incorporate in strategic plan as part of normal management practice. Without it, improvement efforts tend to remain temporary or isolated.","e":"Example: A software team applies CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan while reviewing new-feature release. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"CI lasts only when incorporate in strategic plan becomes part of normal work.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories","CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"CI Principles: Top Management Approval","d":"Unless top management believes that Continuous Improvement is important and unless they actively approve of a Continuous Improvement program, it is unlikely those below them in the hierarchy will give it much attention","g":4,"p":"This principle says continuous improvement must include top management approval as part of normal management practice. Without it, improvement efforts tend to remain temporary or isolated.","e":"Example: A manufacturer applies CI Principles: Top Management Approval while reviewing assembly process. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"CI lasts only when top management approval becomes part of normal work.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of CI Principles: Top Management Approval with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories","CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","d":"The concept of efficiency and improvement must be an integral part of the organizations culture. It must be part of its DNA. X","g":4,"p":"This principle says continuous improvement must include culture of efficiency as part of normal management practice. Without it, improvement efforts tend to remain temporary or isolated.","e":"Example: A university applies CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency while reviewing student registration. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"CI lasts only when culture of efficiency becomes part of normal work.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories","CI Principles: Formal Structure","CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"CI Principles: Create Success Stories","d":"Successes cannot be kept quite. They must be celebrated and shared. X","g":4,"p":"This principle says continuous improvement must include create success stories as part of normal management practice. Without it, improvement efforts tend to remain temporary or isolated.","e":"Example: A restaurant applies CI Principles: Create Success Stories while reviewing meal preparation. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"CI lasts only when create success stories becomes part of normal work.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of CI Principles: Create Success Stories with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","CI Principles: Formal Structure","CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Formal Structure","d":"Many organizations have found that it is necessary to create a formal structure that promotes Continuous Improvement in the organization.","g":4,"p":"This principle says continuous improvement must include formal structure as part of normal management practice. Without it, improvement efforts tend to remain temporary or isolated.","e":"Example: A bank applies CI Principles: Formal Structure while reviewing loan application. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"CI lasts only when formal structure becomes part of normal work.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of CI Principles: Formal Structure with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories","CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","CI Principles: Incorporate In Strategic Plan"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"CI Principles: Measurement","d":"Continuous Improvement projects must measure process outcomes. In this process they collect data and compare existing processes with new or improved ones. X","g":4,"p":"This principle says continuous improvement must include measurement as part of normal management practice. Without it, improvement efforts tend to remain temporary or isolated.","e":"Example: A airline applies CI Principles: Measurement while reviewing baggage handling. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"CI lasts only when measurement becomes part of normal work.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of CI Principles: Measurement with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["CI Principles: Create Success Stories","CI Principles: Culture of Efficiency","CI Principles: Formal Structure"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Eleven Steps to CI","d":"A structured continuous-improvement effort moves from selecting and defining a problem through measurement, mapping, analysis, redesign, implementation, and ongoing control.","g":4,"p":"A structured continuous-improvement effort moves from selecting and defining a problem through measurement, mapping, analysis, redesign, implementation, and ongoing control.","e":"Example: A pharmacy maps prescription filling from request to completion and discovers repeated handoffs, waiting, and unnecessary movement that were hidden in day-to-day work.","m":"Connect Eleven Steps to CI to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Eleven Steps to CI with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark","Eleven Steps: Chart"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities","d":"Opportunities must first be identified. What can help is a Gemba walk. Ideas need to be encouraged and welcomed. Since there are many possibilities, they need to be prioritized before selecting the ones that promise to generate the most benefit to the organization.","g":4,"p":"This is the identify opportunities part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A hotel applies Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities while reviewing room turnover. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Identify Opportunities.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Define Project","d":"A clear and concise project definition is required so that all those involved understand its goals and objectives. But, equally important to focusing on the definition is the need to engage stakeholders at this stage in a Continuous Improvement project.","g":4,"p":"This is the define project part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A delivery company applies Eleven Steps: Define Project while reviewing package routing. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Define Project.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Describe Process","d":"Describe the Suppliers, Inputs, Process steps, Outcomes, and Customers. Flowcharts, such as a Value Stream Map, help to visualize flow through a process and clarify the steps and sequence of steps including any delays that may interfere with process throughput.","g":4,"p":"This is the describe process part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A human-resources team maps employee onboarding from request to completion and discovers repeated handoffs, waiting, and unnecessary movement that were hidden in day-to-day work.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Describe Process.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Measure","d":"Measurement in almost every processes improvement effort is absolutely necessary. It helps to understand how the process is currently performing, define how it will be improved, and is necessary to determine performance once the changes have been implemented. But there is a challenge in the measurement process. They must be both precise and accurate.","g":4,"p":"This is the measure part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing invoice processing, leaders at a finance team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Measure.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Benchmark","d":"Benchmarks are reference points that help set goals and measure progress against these goals. Often benchmarks come from external sources such as competitors or the best practices in an industry.","g":4,"p":"This is the benchmark part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A purchasing team applies Eleven Steps: Benchmark while reviewing purchase-order approval. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Benchmark.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Chart"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Analyze","d":"In this step the data and information that have been collected is shared with stakeholders and analyzed. An important point here is we must be sure that we have found the root cause of a problem and not its symptoms.","g":4,"p":"This is the analyze part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A hospital clinic investigates recurring failures in patient check-in. The team distinguishes the visible symptom from the underlying condition that must be corrected to prevent recurrence.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Analyze.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Benchmark","Eleven Steps: Chart"],"w":"It helps prevent recurring failures by directing attention toward causes, risks, and controls rather than symptoms."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Chart","d":"Charts are visual aids that help us make sense of data. They are quite useful in the Analysis phase. The most useful Charts include histograms, ordered histograms, and fishbone diagrams.","g":4,"p":"This is the chart part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A laboratory builds a fishbone diagram for errors in sample processing, grouping possible causes under people, methods, equipment, materials, measurement, and environment.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Chart.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Design new System","d":"Once the problem has been defined, measurements taken, flow charts drawn and analyzed, the next step is to design the system. In most circumstances it will be well advised to engage those who will eventually use the system.","g":4,"p":"This is the design new system part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A customer-support center applies Eleven Steps: Design new System while reviewing ticket resolution. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Design new System.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Huddle","d":"Very much like the Daily Scrum, the huddle is an opportunity for those engaged in the improvement process to share progress and plans.","g":4,"p":"This is the huddle part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A warehouse applies Eleven Steps: Huddle while reviewing order fulfillment. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Huddle.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Implementation","d":"Once designed, the new process is implemented. Unless those affected by the process have been engaged in its design, resistance-to-change can prove to be a hurdle during the implementation process.","g":4,"p":"This is the implementation part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing new-feature release, leaders at a software team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Implementation.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eleven Steps: Control","d":"A monitoring and control system must be established, complete with control charts, to ensure that the process meets and continues to meet design objectives.","g":4,"p":"This is the control part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A manufacturer plots weekly assembly process results on a control chart. A point beyond the upper limit triggers an investigation into a special cause rather than an immediate redesign of the whole process.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Control.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eleven Steps to CI","Eleven Steps: Analyze","Eleven Steps: Benchmark"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Change Management","d":"The skills associate with addressing the issues of resistance-to-change and managing the change process to achieve a successful outcome where those affected by the change do not actively engage in resistance to new ways of getting work done.","g":4,"p":"Change management is the structured work of helping people understand, adopt, and sustain a new way of working.","e":"Example: Before changing student registration, leaders at a university explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Change Management to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","CI Principles: Top Management Approval","Continuous and Disruptive Change"],"w":"A technically sound solution fails when people do not understand, adopt, or sustain the new way of working."},{"t":"Eight Steps to Change Management","d":"Kotter’s change model builds urgency, forms a guiding coalition, creates and communicates a vision, empowers action, generates short-term wins, consolidates gains, and anchors change in culture.","g":4,"p":"Kotter’s change model builds urgency, forms a guiding coalition, creates and communicates a vision, empowers action, generates short-term wins, consolidates gains, and anchors change in culture.","e":"Example: Before changing meal preparation, leaders at a restaurant explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Eight Steps to Change Management to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps","Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Urgency","d":"Creating a sense of urgency helps to implement change. Research has found that without it, few will be motivated to participate in or accept change. Without urgency, there is no compelling reason why staff should accept the changes that are likely to disrupt the way they work and disrupt the pattern of interactions with others in the organization.","g":4,"p":"This is the urgency part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing loan application, leaders at a bank explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Urgency.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Coalition","d":"Coalitions of stakeholders can also help underscore the need for change. The broader the coalition, the more momentum behind the change. But it cannot be just any group that supports the change. Rather, it must have the power, respect, and motivation to add credibility to the effort","g":4,"p":"This is the coalition part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing baggage handling, leaders at a airline explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Coalition.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Eight Steps: Communicate"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy","d":"Change initiatives must be driven by a strong and clear vision of what will change, and how it will improve performance and how it will prepare the organization for the future.","g":4,"p":"This is the vision and strategy part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing prescription filling, leaders at a pharmacy explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Vision and Strategy.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Communicate","d":"Changes must be communicated to all stakeholders. They must not only understand why the changes are necessary and what changes will be made, but also when they will take place and the procedures followed to implement them.","g":4,"p":"This is the communicate part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing room turnover, leaders at a hotel explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Communicate.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Empower","d":"Empowerment occurs when those who are affected by a change are heard by those responsible for making the change. But it also requires that reasonable suggestions are considered during the change process, and some, not necessarily all, are approved.","g":4,"p":"This is the empower part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing package routing, leaders at a delivery company explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Empower.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Short Term Wins","d":"It is important for the leader to publicly recognize a series of short-term wins or accomplishments. Most importantly, these wins will serve as opportunities to celebrate progress. In addition, they will go a long way in helping to sustain enthusiasm as the change initiative moves forward.","g":4,"p":"This is the short term wins part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing employee onboarding, leaders at a human-resources team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Short Term Wins.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins","Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains","d":"As progress continues, it is often useful for the leader to consolidate the gains that have been made and share them with stakeholders. Often, these gains can be used to motivate even more gains.","g":4,"p":"This is the consolidate gains part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: A finance team applies Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains while reviewing invoice processing. The concept helps the team decide what evidence to collect, what action to take, and how to verify that performance improves.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Consolidate Gains.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","d":"Culture changes slowly, but it can be changed. When change initiatives are successfully completed, it is important for leaders to “anchor” those successes to the existing culture of the organization. By anchoring the success of these changes to culture, the values and beliefs of the organization become stronger and more widely accepted. Future changes are likely to receive more support, and resistance will fall.","g":4,"p":"This is the anchor to culture part of the improvement sequence. The team completes this work deliberately before moving forward so later decisions are based on evidence rather than assumptions.","e":"Example: Before changing purchase-order approval, leaders at a purchasing team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Remember this as one checkpoint in the sequence: Anchor to Culture.","x":"Do not memorize the label alone. Know what work is completed at this step and what evidence is needed before moving on.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Coalition","Eight Steps: Communicate"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Theory of Constraints","d":"The Theory of Constraints was created by Eliyahu Goldratt. In almost every situation there are constraints that prevent us from reaching our goals. The Theory of Constraints is an approach that helps us recognize and overcome them.","g":4,"p":"The Theory of Constraints says overall system performance is limited by its most restrictive constraint. Improving nonconstraints may not improve total output.","e":"A lab can prepare 80 samples per day but one analyzer can process only 45. Adding staff to preparation creates more queue; improving the analyzer increases total throughput.","m":"The whole chain moves only as fast as its constraint.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Theory of Constraints with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Identify Systems Constraints","Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints","Elevate the System’s Constraints"],"w":"Improving the true constraint increases total system performance, while improving other steps may only create excess work."},{"t":"Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps","d":"The five focusing steps are identify the constraint, exploit it, subordinate other work to it, elevate it, and repeat when the constraint moves.","g":4,"p":"The five focusing steps are identify the constraint, exploit it, subordinate other work to it, elevate it, and repeat when the constraint moves.","e":"Example: A laboratory finds that one approval step limits the total flow of sample processing. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Connect Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Theory of Constraints","Theory of Constraints: Benefits","Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Identify Systems Constraints","d":"The first step is to identify the constraints in a process. They might be, delays, inadequate staff, slow machines, and manifest themselves as backups and bottlenecks. Value Stream maps at this stage can be very helpful. Another useful tool I the Kanban chart. The Eight Wastes may also impose constraints on process throughput.","g":4,"p":"Find the step that most limits the system's ability to achieve its goal. The constraint is the point where improvement has the greatest leverage.","e":"Example: A customer-support center uses To Do, Doing, and Done columns for ticket resolution and limits the Doing column to five items. New work is pulled only when capacity opens.","m":"Connect Identify Systems Constraints to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Identify Systems Constraints with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints","Elevate the System’s Constraints","Eleven Steps: Identify Opportunities"],"w":"It turns process information into a visual pattern, helping the team recognize what deserves attention and choose an appropriate response."},{"t":"Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints","d":"What is the best and most accessible mean of eliminating the constraint. Often this requires that we make a quick improvement. Sometimes additional training can help.","g":4,"p":"Exploit the constraint by using its existing capacity as effectively as possible before spending money to expand it.","e":"Example: A warehouse finds that one approval step limits the total flow of order fulfillment. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Connect Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Elevate the System’s Constraints","Eleven Steps: Design new System","Identify Systems Constraints"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision.","d":"Make sure that the focus is on the most binding constraint. Further other activities and steps in the process must be analyzed to ensure that they are aligned with and support the constraint.","g":4,"p":"Align other activities to support the constraint. Do not allow nonconstraints to overload, starve, or distract the bottleneck.","e":"Example: A software team finds that one approval step limits the total flow of new-feature release. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Connect Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision. to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision. with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Identify Systems Constraints","Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints","Elevate the System’s Constraints"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Elevate the System’s Constraints","d":"If the constraint persists, then further action must be taken to eliminate it. The pressure must continue until the constraint no longer affects process throughput. This may mean adding more machines, replacing suppliers, scheduling more shifts, redesigning web sites, and scheduling more training sessions..","g":4,"p":"If the constraint still limits performance after exploitation and subordination, increase its capacity through investment, redesign, staffing, or technology.","e":"Example: A manufacturer finds that one approval step limits the total flow of assembly process. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Connect Elevate the System’s Constraints to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Elevate the System’s Constraints with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints","Eleven Steps: Design new System","Identify Systems Constraints"],"w":"It helps describe process performance accurately so teams do not make decisions from averages alone."},{"t":"Go Back When Necessary","d":"After one constraint has been addressed another constraint will inevitably affect process throughput. So, in the interest of continuous improvement, it would be appropriate to refocus on the new constraint.","g":4,"p":"Once one constraint is broken, another part of the system becomes the new constraint. Repeat the focusing steps and avoid allowing old policies to become the bottleneck.","e":"Example: A university finds that one approval step limits the total flow of student registration. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Connect Go Back When Necessary to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Go Back When Necessary with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Subordinate Everything Else to the Above Decision.","Elevate the System’s Constraints","Theory of Constraints: Benefits"],"w":"It helps reveal where work slows down, consumes resources, or fails to create customer value."},{"t":"Theory of Constraints: Benefits","d":"Less ambiguous to identify what it is that is interfering with achieving process performance goals. Places the focus on exactly where the shortfall in process performance has the most impact on addressing customer needs and achieving organizational profits. Provides a focus for a program of continuous improvement. Takes an overall or systems approach to isolating where backups occur. Encourages using what you already have to mitigate the constraint rather than engaging in costly capital or workforce expenditures such as larger facilities, better equipment or additional personnel.","g":4,"p":"Less ambiguous to identify what it is that is interfering with achieving process performance goals.","e":"Example: A restaurant finds that one approval step limits the total flow of meal preparation. It improves that bottleneck first because speeding up nonconstraints would only create a larger queue.","m":"Connect Theory of Constraints: Benefits to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Do not confuse the definition of Theory of Constraints: Benefits with evidence that it has been applied correctly. Look for the required data, behavior, or decision.","r":["Theory of Constraints","Theory of Constraints: Five Focusing Steps","Decide How to Exploit the System’s Constraints"],"w":"It keeps improvement connected to measurable customer needs instead of internal activity that may not create value."},{"t":"Resistance to Change","d":"Reluctance or interference by anyone in the organization to changes in process methods and steps.","g":4,"p":"Resistance to change is often a response to uncertainty, loss of control, lack of trust, competing priorities, or a belief that the change will not help.","e":"Example: Before changing loan application, leaders at a bank explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Resistance to Change to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps","Change Management","Continuous and Disruptive Change"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps","d":"Resistance can be reduced by building urgency, forming a coalition, clarifying the vision, communicating, empowering employees, celebrating short-term wins, consolidating gains, and anchoring the change in culture.","g":4,"p":"Resistance can be reduced by building urgency, forming a coalition, clarifying the vision, communicating, empowering employees, celebrating short-term wins, consolidating gains, and anchoring the change in culture.","e":"Example: Before changing baggage handling, leaders at a airline explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Resistance-to-Change: Eight Steps to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps to Change Management","Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Eight Steps: Coalition"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Urgency","d":"A sense of urgency helps. Research has found that without it, few will be motivated to participate in or accept change. Without urgency, there is no compelling reason why staff should accept the changes that are likely to disrupt the way they work and disrupt the pattern of interactions with others in the organization.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using urgency to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing prescription filling, leaders at a pharmacy explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Urgency to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Coalition","Overcoming Resistance: Communicate"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Coalition","d":"Coalitions of stakeholders can also help underscore the need for change. They can be instrumental in providing the momentum necessary to break down the barriers of resistance. The broader the coalition, the more momentum behind the change.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using coalition to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing room turnover, leaders at a hotel explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Coalition to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Communicate","Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Vision and Strategy","d":"Change initiatives must be driven by a strong and clear vision of what will change, how it will improve performance, and how it will prepare the organization for the future.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using vision and strategy to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing package routing, leaders at a delivery company explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Vision and Strategy to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Vision and Strategy","Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Coalition"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Communicate","d":"Changes must be communicated to all stakeholders. They must not only understand why the changes are necessary and what changes will be made, but also when they will take place and the procedures followed to implement them.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using communicate to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing employee onboarding, leaders at a human-resources team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Communicate to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Coalition","Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Empower Employees","d":"Empowerment occurs when those who are affected by a change are heard by those responsible for making the change. But it also requires that reasonable suggestions are considered during the change process, and some, not necessarily all, are approved.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using empower employees to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing invoice processing, leaders at a finance team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Empower Employees to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Coalition","Overcoming Resistance: Communicate"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins","d":"It is important for the leader to publicly recognize a series of short-term wins or accomplishments. Most importantly, these wins will serve as opportunities to celebrate progress. In addition, they will go a long way in helping to sustain enthusiasm as the change initiative moves forward.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using short-term wins to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing purchase-order approval, leaders at a purchasing team explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Short-Term Wins to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Short Term Wins","Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Coalition"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains","d":"As progress continues, it is often useful for the leader to consolidate the gains that have been made and share them with stakeholders. Often, these gains can be used to motivate even more gains.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using consolidate gains to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing patient check-in, leaders at a hospital clinic explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Consolidate Gains to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Consolidate Gains","Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Coalition"],"w":"It gives the improvement team a practical way to understand, measure, or improve an important part of process performance."},{"t":"Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture","d":"When change initiatives are successfully completed, it is important for leaders to “anchor” those successes to the existing culture of the organization. When anchored, the values and beliefs of the organization become stronger and more widely accepted.","g":4,"p":"This step reduces resistance by using anchor to culture to help people understand, participate in, and sustain the change.","e":"Example: Before changing sample processing, leaders at a laboratory explain why the change is needed, involve respected employees, remove obstacles, and publicize early wins so the new method lasts.","m":"Connect Overcoming Resistance: Anchor to Culture to its practical purpose: what problem it reveals, prevents, measures, or improves.","x":"Communication alone is not change management. Adoption also requires participation, capability, reinforcement, and removal of barriers.","r":["Eight Steps: Anchor to Culture","Overcoming Resistance: Coalition","Overcoming Resistance: Communicate"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"Teams in Name Only","d":"A group of people working together in a conventional or traditional way and under the watchful eye of a team leader or manager is often a team in name only.","g":4,"p":"A team in name only is a group labeled as a team without shared goals, complementary work, mutual accountability, or real collaboration.","e":"Example: A customer-support center forms a cross-functional group to improve ticket resolution. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams","d":"A ‘real’ Continuous Improvement team, however, which we might refer to as a high-performance team, is a group of people with multiple and complementary skills who are self-directed, strongly motivated to accomplish challenging goals, and committed to improving efficiency while at the same time, also focusing on customer value and customer satisfaction.","g":4,"p":"A high-performance team combines a clear purpose, specific goals, complementary skills, disciplined ways of working, and mutual accountability.","e":"Example: A warehouse forms a cross-functional group to improve order fulfillment. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","Teams in Name Only","High-Performance Teams: Mutually Accountable"],"w":"Complex improvement work requires coordinated skills, trust, and shared accountability."},{"t":"Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","d":"High-performance teams share a common purpose, set clear performance goals, combine complementary skills, act with discipline, and hold members mutually accountable.","g":4,"p":"High-performance teams share a common purpose, set clear performance goals, combine complementary skills, act with discipline, and hold members mutually accountable.","e":"Example: A software team forms a cross-functional group to improve new-feature release. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose","High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Disciplined Action","d":"High-performance Continuous Improvement teams are capable of disciplined action. They are disciplined in establishing a common purpose, establishing performance goals, and holding each other to mutually agreed-upon standards of performance. They are disciplined in following a Democratic style of engagement and following this approach to organize and plan every step in delivering results.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on disciplined action to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A manufacturer forms a cross-functional group to improve assembly process. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose","d":"Members of the team sharing a common goal. Without it they can work at cross-purposes and outcomes suffer.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on common purpose to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A university forms a cross-functional group to improve student registration. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Performance Goals","d":"Team decides on its own performance goals.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on performance goals to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A restaurant forms a cross-functional group to improve meal preparation. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Complementary Skills","d":"Rather than similar skills, high-performance teams need to include individuals with diverse traits, skills, interests, and backgrounds. It should be a team of people who think differently.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on complementary skills to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A bank forms a cross-functional group to improve loan application. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."},{"t":"High-Performance Teams: Mutually Accountable","d":"Holding team members responsible for achieving project goals.","g":4,"p":"This characteristic means the team relies on mutually accountable to turn a group of capable people into a coordinated unit that consistently delivers results.","e":"Example: A airline forms a cross-functional group to improve baggage handling. Members agree on one goal, contribute different expertise, and hold each other responsible for the result.","m":"A real team shares purpose, work, and accountability, not just a meeting invitation.","x":"A collection of high performers is not automatically a high-performance team.","r":["Characteristics of High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams","High-Performance Teams: Common Purpose"],"w":"Improvement depends on behavior as well as tools. This concept helps people align, adopt new practices, and sustain results."}]);
